# Exam track — Theme: Describing a Picture — Sub-lesson 1: General Description
#
# The Sproochentest speaking exam includes a picture-description task: the
# examiner shows a photo and the candidate describes it. This theme drills that
# task for ONE photo: a busy Luxembourgish funfair (Schueberfouer-style) on a
# sunny summer afternoon — a Ferris wheel and a "Ghost Rider" ride in the
# background, food and game stalls left and right, a family (woman + boy)
# walking in the foreground, two girls in black clothes on the right, blue sky
# with a few clouds.
#
# The photo's coverage is split across three sub-lessons, one per exam task:
#   01 — WHAT: the elements in the scene, their relative positions, what the
#        people are doing.                          ← this file
#   02 — WHO:  the people, 1–2 of them described visually.
#   03 — WHEN: the weather, and a hedged guess at the time of year.
#
# PURE DESCRIPTION. No @question examiner prompts and no first-person opinion,
# preference or feeling — those belong to the topic themes (vacation, shopping,
# family). A depicted person's *visible* emotion is fine: that is observation.
# Rule of thumb — if a stranger could not check the sentence against the photo,
# it does not belong here.
#
# Cut in Aug 2026 for failing exactly that test: `Op der Fouer ass vill Musek.`
# (a photo has no sound), `D'Leit sinn all frou an hu Spaass.` (`hu Spaass` is
# an inference about an internal state, and `all` generalises past what is
# visible), and the fries/cotton-candy stall, whose contents are not legible in
# the frame. Nothing about the *learner* going to the fair was ever here.
#
# Vocabulary and grammar are held to A1–A2 deliberately (present tense, simple
# main clauses, spatial adverbs + prepositions, no attributive adjectives on
# masculine nouns). This is stricter than the ~B1 guideline the other themes
# use — the picture task is where beginners can score, so the sentences stay
# assemblable.
#
# The three @fill blocks carry the frames that recur across every picture task
# (existential "a lot is going on", "in the background I see …", "in the
# foreground I see …") — the fixed frame teaches the pattern, the blank drills
# the positional or scene word. See the skill's references/content-contract.md.

@lesson P1.01 "Schueberfouer: General Description"

# The photo all three picture sub-lessons describe. Pre-cropped to 16:9 and
# encoded as WebP at 880px — see .claude/memory/picture-description-theme.md for
# the pipeline. @image-alt stays: it is the a11y text, and the caption of the
# placeholder frame if the file is ever missing.
@image "/assets/exam/picture/schueberfouer/img/schueberfouer.webp"
@image-alt "A busy funfair on a sunny afternoon: a Ferris wheel and a rollercoaster in the background, food and game stalls on the left and right, a woman and a boy walking in the foreground, blue sky with a few clouds."

# --- Talking about a picture ---
@word d'Bild = the picture
@word de Vierdergrond = the foreground
@word den Hannergrond = the background
@word lénks = on the left
@word riets = on the right
@word uewen = at the top
@word ënnen = at the bottom
@word gesinn = to see
@word kucken = to look

# --- At the fair ---
@word d'Fouer = the fair
@word d'Riserad = the Ferris wheel
@word d'Buden = the stalls
@word d'Attraktiounen = the rides

# --- People ---
@word d'Leit = the people
@word d'Famill = the family
@word de Jong = the boy
@word d'Meedchen = the girl
@word d'Fra = the woman

# --- Doing things ---
@word trëppelen = to walk
@word stoen = to stand
@word ongeféier = approximately

@sentence
@lu Op dem Bild gesinn ech vill Leit.
@en In the picture I see a lot of people.
@distractor-lu keng
@distractor-lu Buden
@distractor-en no
@distractor-en stalls

@sentence
@lu Am Vierdergrond trëppelt eng Fra mat engem Jong.
@en In the foreground a woman is walking with a boy.
@distractor-lu Meedchen
@distractor-lu steet
@distractor-en girl
@distractor-en stands

@sentence
@lu Lénks a riets stinn d'Buden.
@en On the left and on the right there are the stalls.
@distractor-lu uewen
@distractor-lu ënnen
@distractor-en top
@distractor-en bottom

@sentence
@lu Ech gesinn d'Attraktiounen uewen an d'Leit ënnen.
@en I see the rides at the top and the people at the bottom.
@distractor-lu lénks
@distractor-lu riets
@distractor-en left
@distractor-en right

@sentence
@lu Ech gesinn ongeféier fënnef Leit am Vierdergrond.
@en I see about five people in the foreground.
@distractor-lu Hannergrond
@distractor-lu zwee
@distractor-en background
@distractor-en two

@sentence
@lu D'Leit trëppelen a kucken d'Attraktiounen.
@en The people are walking and looking at the rides.
@distractor-lu stinn
@distractor-lu Buden
@distractor-en standing
@distractor-en stalls

# --- Reusable frames (@fill) ---

# The existential opener. `vill lass` is the idiom for "a lot going on" (LOD
# `lass` sense 4), and it transfers to any busy scene, which is what makes it a
# frame rather than a sentence about this photo. Both distractors describe the
# opposite scene, so neither works in the blank.
@fill
@lu Um Bild ass vill [lass].
@en In the picture a lot is [happening].
@distractor-lu roueg
@distractor-lu zou
@distractor-en quiet
@distractor-en closed

# The background frame. Two blanks of different word classes (verb, then noun)
# so they cannot swap — R6. The determiner `d'` stays in the frame, which is
# what rules out masculine `Bam` and `Vierdergrond` on grammar alone (R4).
@fill
@lu Am Hannergrond [gesinn] ech d'[Riserad].
@en In the background I [see] the [Ferris wheel].
@distractor-lu Vierdergrond
@distractor-lu Bam
@distractor-en foreground
@distractor-en tree

# The mirror frame for the foreground — the positional word is the blank here,
# since that is the pair the examiner listens for. Both distractors are
# grammatical in the slot and factually wrong about the photo.
@fill
@lu Am [Vierdergrond] gesinn ech eng Famill.
@en In the [foreground] I see a family.
@distractor-lu Hannergrond
@distractor-lu Riserad
@distractor-en background
@distractor-en Ferris wheel
